---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ContentGrid from "../components/structure/ContentGrid.astro";
import ContentSection from "../components/structure/ContentSection.astro";
import PeopleGroup from "../components/content/PeopleGroup.astro";
import PageHeading from "../components/structure/PageHeading.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/structure/PageBody.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/structure/SiteHeader.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the Telecommunications History Group | Connections Museum"
  description="Learn about the Telecommunications History Group, its mission, staff, board, partners, and sponsors."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <PageBody>
    <PageHeading level="h1">About Us</PageHeading>
    <ContentGrid columns={2}>
      <r-cell span="row">
        <span id="thg" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <ContentSection id="what-is-thg" title="What is the Telecommunications History Group (THG)?" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            The Connections Museum is part of the Telecommunications History Group
            Inc., a 501(c)(3) non-profit organization which proudly preserves and
            shares the rich heritage of an industry that has had and continues
            to have a huge impact on our society, our culture, and most of all,
            our ability to communicate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why is this important?</strong> The telecommunications
            industry created the roots of modern communication technologies,
            making possible the development and evolution of radio, TV, and
            computers, in addition to telephones in all their shapes and
            functions. This history creates a bridge between the past and
            present.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are passionate about sharing well-known and little-known facts,
            stories, equipment, and documentation related to the
            telecommunications industry with industry insiders, history buffs,
            sociological researchers, students, and the general public. We
            believe our history reflects not only key milestones in
            communication but also a living and continually changing record of
            our lives, the way we conduct business, and our culture as a whole.
          </p>
          <p><i>Whether you realize it or not, the telecommunications industry has touched and influenced your life!</i></p>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="what-we-do" title="What We Do" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            Whatever your motivation, we’re here to share what we know, what we
            have, and where we’ve been in relation to telecommunications
            history. Whether visiting one of our locations, browsing our
            website to learn about telecom history, looking for a specific
            piece of information, or looking for others to connect with who
            share similar interests, we are here for you.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are a former employee of a telecommunications company who
            likes to relive the glory days or share history with your friends
            and family, you will be pleasantly surprised at how much information
            we have gathered and organized. Historical photographs and news
            articles tell many stories of innovation, technology, culture, and
            of course, people. Seeing the actual telephones and related
            equipment will take you back in time and allow you to place yourself
            in this rich history.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are not a phone company “insider,” you may still be
            fascinated by the history of telecommunications, both the more
            public side and the behind-the-scenes stories. Whether you want to
            reminisce about the telephones and telephone directories you used
            to have, or simply gain a broader understanding of what led up to
            the current technology of the cell phone in your pocket, we have a
            lot to share.
          </p>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="mission" title="Our Mission" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            The mission of the Connections Museum and the Telecommunications History Group is to acquire,
            preserve, and promote the rich heritage of the telecommunications
            industry and to connect that past to evolving technologies and
            cultures.
          </p>
          <h3>In furtherance of this mission, THG will:</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Collect and preserve writings, recordings, videos, movies, records, and pictures that provide insight into telecommunications history.</li>
            <li>Collect and preserve artifacts and objects important to the evolution of telephony and telecommunications.</li>
            <li>Research and record information, facts, and folklore important to the history of telecommunication.</li>
            <li>Organize and make accessible to the public the resources and objects acquired by THG.</li>
            <li>Promulgate and disseminate telecommunications history and be a resource for those interested in it.</li>
            <li>Demonstrate how the telecommunications industry and its people have contributed to the social, cultural, economic, and technological development of this country.</li>
          </ul>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
    </ContentGrid>

    <ContentSection
      id="staff-and-board"
      title="THG Staff and Board"
      headingLevel="h2"
    >
      <p>
        The Telecommunications History Group, Inc., is lucky to have an amazing
        and dedicated group of staff and board members. Board members serve as
        volunteers, working with staff and other volunteers to set strategy and
        goals and ensure those goals are implemented.
      </p>
      <p>
        While several THG Board members are directly related to the
        telecommunications industry as retired or current phone company
        employees, additional members bring expertise in areas such as
        marketing, fundraising, and law. The Board has representatives chiefly
        from Denver and Seattle, as well as other locations across the United
        States.
      </p>
      <ContentGrid columns={3}>
        <r-cell>
          <PeopleGroup
            id="staff"
            title="Staff"
            headingLevel="h3"
            people={[
              { name: "Renee Lang", title: "Managing Director" },
            ]}
          />
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <PeopleGroup
            id="board-officers"
            title="Board Officers"
            headingLevel="h3"
            people={[
              { name: "Peter Amstein", title: "THG President", description: "Software Industry Executive (Ret.)" },
              { name: "Michael W. Nearing", title: "THG Vice President", description: "Network Engineer (Ret.)" },
              { name: "R. William (Bill) Johnston", title: "THG Treasurer", description: "SVP & Controller, Qwest Communications (Ret.)" },
              { name: "Sarah Autumn", title: "THG Secretary", description: "Instructor, F5 Networks" },
            ]}
          />
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <PeopleGroup
            id="board-members"
            title="Board Members"
            headingLevel="h3"
            people={[
              { name: "David Burgess", description: "ZaiNar, Inc." },
              { name: "Jody L. Georgeson", description: "US West (Ret.)" },
              { name: "Ed Mattson", description: "Medical Director (Ret.)" },
              { name: "Roger Christensen", description: "Executive VP & Chief Administrative Officer (Ret.) – Media One" },
              { name: "Philip A. Linse", description: "Lumen" },
              { name: "Mary Retka", description: "Vice President, Industry Relations & Public Policy – SOMOS" },
            ]}
          />
        </r-cell>
      </ContentGrid>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection id="contact-thg" title="Contact THG" headingLevel="h2">
      <p>
        If you have a question or some matter that you'd like to address
        directly to our board, you can email them at
        <a href="mailto:board@telecomhistory.group">board@telecomhistory.group</a>
        and someone will respond as they're able.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentSection id="partners-and-sponsors" title="Partners & Sponsors" headingLevel="h2">
      <p>
        Thank you to our corporate sponsors Lumen, CenturyLink, CBM Network
        Services, and the Pioneers Volunteer Network.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operations of the Telecommunications History Group are made possible
        through donations of money, physical space, and time from both
        individuals and companies. THG is not owned by or a division of any
        corporation, although we are housed in CenturyLink buildings, which are
        generously provided by CenturyLink. We appreciate our sponsors and
        could not share this important history without them.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>
  </PageBody>
</BaseLayout>
